1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:06,733
Is the difference real, and what drives it?

2
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:13,621
By default, tests and regressions read the raw records that survive the filters, in the same scan.

3
00:00:14,421 --> 00:00:19,677
Post-tests are the second tier. They run after windows, on the finished result rows.

4
00:00:20,477 --> 00:00:26,309
A test names a type, a field and often a split; a regression, a target and its predictors.

5
00:00:27,109 --> 00:00:32,706
Results land in their own sections. The cohort has two hundred and forty illustrative rows.

6
00:00:33,506 --> 00:00:42,346
Every test returns the same shape: a statistic, degrees of freedom, a two-sided p-value, its alpha, and whether the null was rejected.

7
00:00:43,146 --> 00:00:47,698
The details carry the rest: the difference, its interval and an effect size.

8
00:00:48,498 --> 00:00:53,327
Read both. A tiny p-value with a tiny effect is still a tiny effect.

9
00:00:54,127 --> 00:01:02,434
Start from the outcome: a number across groups, the same subjects twice, a yes or no rate, two categories, two numbers, or a shape.

10
00:01:03,234 --> 00:01:07,253
Skew points to the rank tests, and unequal spread to the Welch forms.

11
00:01:08,053 --> 00:01:13,074
The t-test asks whether the gap between the two arms' mean spend is bigger than chance.

12
00:01:13,874 --> 00:01:21,583
It uses Welch's form, so it never assumes equal spread. B spends about seven more, and the effect size is moderate.

13
00:01:22,383 --> 00:01:29,666
With skewed values, Mann-Whitney compares ranks instead. It asks a different question, so disagreement is information.

14
00:01:30,466 --> 00:01:34,933
With three or more groups, one-way ANOVA asks whether any of the means differ.

15
00:01:35,733 --> 00:01:43,677
Here it says no: region explains about one percent of spend. A rejection would only say that some means differ, not which.

16
00:01:44,477 --> 00:01:54,597
Check normality and spread first. Unequal spread calls for Welch's ANOVA, skew for Kruskal-Wallis, and Tukey, as a post-test, finds which pairs differ.

17
00:01:55,397 --> 00:02:02,872
Chi-square tests whether two categorical fields are independent, here region and arm. Labels sort alphabetically.

18
00:02:03,672 --> 00:02:07,051
It compares each count with what independence would predict.

19
00:02:07,851 --> 00:02:14,195
The p-value says the pattern is unlikely by chance, but Cramér's V says the association is weak.

20
00:02:14,995 --> 00:02:21,680
An expected count under five draws a warning; for a small two-by-two table, use Fisher's exact test.

21
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:30,403
First, check the split. Chi-square against expected shares, a goodness-of-fit test, finds no sample-ratio mismatch.

22
00:02:31,203 --> 00:02:37,760
Name the control as the reference, and the difference reads variant minus control: B spends seven more than A.

23
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:45,971
Add a pre-period covariate, and CUPED removes the variance it explains. The interval narrows to about a third of its width.

24
00:02:46,771 --> 00:02:51,942
With groups set, every test runs once per group, and each result carries its group key.

25
00:02:52,742 --> 00:02:58,702
East and North clear the bar. South, with sixty-nine rows, just misses. Grouped tests buffer.

26
00:02:59,502 --> 00:03:03,926
Pearson's r measures how tightly two numbers move together in a straight line.

27
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:08,574
Spend and visits correlate strongly, at about point seven eight.

28
00:03:09,374 --> 00:03:14,971
Pearson sees only straight lines, and outliers sway it. Spearman and Kendall use ranks.

29
00:03:15,771 --> 00:03:21,176
Post-tests run last, on the result rows: after groups, aggregations and windows.

30
00:03:21,976 --> 00:03:28,512
The trend test asks whether an ordered series rises or falls; a moving average first tames the noise.

31
00:03:29,312 --> 00:03:37,064
Tukey compares every pair of group means with family-wise control. Result rows carry no weights, so it never runs weighted.

32
00:03:37,864 --> 00:03:45,253
Run many tests and some will look significant by luck. Add a multiplicity block, and Pulse corrects them as a family.

33
00:03:46,053 --> 00:03:50,093
The adjusted p-value sits beside the raw one, which never moves.

34
00:03:50,893 --> 00:03:56,362
Holm sorts them, multiplies the smallest by four, the next by three, and keeps their order.

35
00:03:57,162 --> 00:04:02,717
Pick a method and a family. A regression's coefficients join only through their own block.

36
00:04:03,517 --> 00:04:08,026
Ten or more uncorrected p-values make predict raise an advisory.

37
00:04:08,826 --> 00:04:14,509
Ordinary least squares fits a straight line through the data. Here, spend from visits and tenure.

38
00:04:15,309 --> 00:04:20,693
Each residual is the gap from a point to the fit, and least squares minimizes their squares.

39
00:04:21,493 --> 00:04:26,600
Each extra visit adds about three point one five to spend, holding tenure fixed.

40
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:36,837
It explains about seventy percent of the variation. A null, not-a-number or infinite value drops the row, and a plain fit streams in one pass.

41
00:04:37,637 --> 00:04:44,408
A robust covariance, like H C 3, swaps in sandwich standard errors, and the coefficients stay put.

42
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:51,659
A holdout fits on some rows and scores the rest. Out of sample, the error rises a little, as it should.

43
00:04:52,459 --> 00:04:54,878
Either option forces a buffered run.

44
00:04:55,678 --> 00:05:03,985
One fit covers many textbook names. Penalties give ridge, lasso and elastic net; the last two report no standard errors.

45
00:05:04,785 --> 00:05:09,785
Resampling replaces the standard errors with jackknife or bootstrap estimates.

46
00:05:10,585 --> 00:05:18,273
Selection searches the predictors by AIC or BIC. A dropped predictor vanishes: absent is not zero.

47
00:05:19,073 --> 00:05:24,009
For a curve, build polynomial columns upstream, then fit a straight line through them.

48
00:05:24,809 --> 00:05:29,958
For a yes or no outcome, the generalized linear model fits a logistic curve.

49
00:05:30,758 --> 00:05:38,574
Its coefficients are on the log-odds scale. Each extra visit multiplies the odds of converting by about one point four.

50
00:05:39,374 --> 00:05:47,723
It always buffers, because the fit iterates. When a predictor splits the outcome perfectly, Pulse refuses the fit as separated.

51
00:05:48,523 --> 00:05:55,720
That slot fails alone: it carries the error, the response warns, and the least-squares fit beside it still returns.

52
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:00,752
The Bayesian linear fit starts from a prior and updates it with the data.

53
00:06:01,552 --> 00:06:08,301
It reports credible intervals and no p-values at all. With a vague prior, it lands on the least-squares answer.

54
00:06:09,101 --> 00:06:14,997
Tests built on running counts and moments stream, and so do plain least-squares and Bayesian fits.

55
00:06:15,797 --> 00:06:25,405
Rank tests, exact tables, iterative fits and media-mix models buffer, as do resampling, selection, robust errors, holdouts and grouped tests.

56
00:06:26,205 --> 00:06:31,888
A mean-based test beside a Welford aggregate on the same field reuses its running state.

57
00:06:32,688 --> 00:06:41,528
That's the toolkit today: tests that compare, relate and check shape, multivariate tests, and regression models, media-mix models among them.

58
00:06:42,328 --> 00:06:52,341
More are added over time, and embedders can register their own tests and regressions through the extensions option. The manifest shows what your instance offers.

59
00:06:53,141 --> 00:06:58,034
Remember: read the effect size beside the p-value. Next up, matrices.
